re of meaning and the distinction between words and things. His theory of signification, as outlined in *Summa Logicae*, influenced later developments in conceptual semantics.\n\n7. **John Locke (1632–1704)** – A key figure in the Enlightenment, Locke’s *An Essay Concerning Human Understanding* provided a foundational account of semantics, emphasizing the role of experience in the formation of meaning. His theory of ideas and their relation to language was highly influential.\n\n8. **Immanuel Kant (1748–1804)** – A central figure in modern philosophy, Kant’s *Critique of Pure Reason* addressed the nature of meaning and the limits of human knowledge. His work on the a priori structures of understanding had a profound impact on later developments in conceptual semantics.\n\n9. **Friedrich Nietzsche (1844–1900)** – A controversial figure in philosophy, Nietzsche’s works such as *On the Genealogy of Morals* and *The Will to Power* challenged traditional views of meaning and language. His critique of metaphysical language and the idea of the will to power reshaped modern semantics.\n\n10. **Ludwig Wittgenstein (1889–1951)** – A pivotal figure in 20th-century philosophy, Wittgenstein’s *Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus* and *Philosophical Investigations* transformed the field of semantics. His work on the limits of language and the nature of meaning has had a lasting impact on formal and conceptual semantics.\n\nThese authors and works represent a range of influential contributions to semantics from the Classical antiquity and the Middle Ages, focusing on Lexical, Formal, and Conceptual semantics, and are recognized for their foundational or controversial theories within a Western cultural/linguistic context.",
